Cell[TextData[{
 "So \[Delta] is an invariant tensor of so(3). This is somewhat trivial, \
since one definition of the Lie group SO(3) to begin with is that the length \
\[Delta]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["", "ij"], TraditionalForm]],
  FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->
  "2bbaea63-ca20-4665-9219-16262e0d3b61"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["v", "i"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a9c5bcb9-107e-4eec-bf75-da1436b8ca48"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["v", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "71adcac1-f137-4023-bde5-41b1a4d392d5"],
 " is invariant. This leads to condition that the rotation matrices are \
orthogonal, which in turn implies that the generators (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "m"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "16207ac6-7978-43f5-b0d4-d3f60a5fe7a2"],
 ")",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox[
    SubscriptBox["", "ij"],
    FontSlant->"Italic"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4f184ed5-261d-4002-815a-e6f0e4712dc6"],
 " are real and antisymmetric in ",
 StyleBox["i,j. ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "The point is that if we turn the logic around and start from the algebra of \
real antisymmetric matrices (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["L", "m"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "bbf1c793-a5d3-4393-8240-ba2b9388f12b"],
 ")",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox[
    SubscriptBox["", "ij"],
    FontSlant->"Italic"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "47d5d2cd-e0ea-45d4-9682-937c5bd4e9dc"],
 ", we then have to \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]discover\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
that we have an invariant tensor \[Delta]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["", "ij"], TraditionalForm]],
  FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->
  "658dd1a8-5295-4bde-9a97-ee96dc8d7481"],
 ". The motivation for turning the logic around is that the concept of \
rotation (even in higher dimension) are very particular types of geometric \
transformation, but the concept of algebra generalizes to completely \
different types of transformations. ",
 "\n\nNote the two different \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]identity matrices\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] id vs. \[Delta]: \[LineSeparator]id \[Element] \
End(",
 StyleBox["V",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ") =  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["V", TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "b1f8e11f-5b60-4e05-b622-7357864fcbc4"],
 "\[TensorProduct] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["V", "*"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "87fc756d-b443-427a-8e16-1370eec0edb6"],
 ", input: vector, output: vector  id(",
 StyleBox["v",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ") = ",
 StyleBox["v, ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "or ",
 StyleBox["(id)",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    SuperscriptBox["", "i"], "j"], TraditionalForm]],
  FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->
  "8bb727b5-7f7c-4285-b119-e13b5ee60a27"],
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["v", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "3c9b044f-4850-4398-b640-b3a43dd3f31e"],
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"=", " ", 
    SuperscriptBox["v", "i"]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5a5b70e1-20c5-41c5-a2a0-9f3d1ca9ad7f"],
 StyleBox["\[LineSeparator] ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "(this is really a linear map as in linear algebra: it maps vectors to \
vectors)\[LineSeparator]\[Delta] \[Element] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["V", "*"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "c0c78f3d-9ff6-461f-b8a6-2685ccbeb880"],
 "\[TensorProduct] ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["V", "*"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "1f5b97a2-aa3e-4917-ad63-451a9a3527dc"],
 ",  input: two vectors, output: number =  \[Delta]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["", "ij"], TraditionalForm]],
  FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->
  "c04bd4c8-b9ae-4aa5-b740-00ef970244a9"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["v", "i"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2a77db9d-2920-42aa-9d76-9d89e8732b6a"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["v", "j"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "cb699b23-dbb1-4fb3-ae8c-107011361cea"],
 "  (or opposite for ",
 StyleBox["V",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " ",
 "\[LeftRightArrow] ",
 StyleBox["V*",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ")\n (here \[Delta]",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["", "ij"], TraditionalForm]],
  FontSlant->"Italic",ExpressionUUID->
  "a0e0eb55-df1a-4dd1-974e-a682273ccb7a"],
 " is a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]coefficient matrix\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], \
i.e. a collection of numbers, but we do ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " use it to map vectors to vectors)"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.976697374737359*^9, 3.976697385091051*^9}, {
  3.976697421853594*^9, 3.976697456062622*^9}, {3.976697587234458*^9, 
  3.9766977036226797`*^9}, {3.976697742971147*^9, 3.976697837801634*^9}, {
  3.9766983993848057`*^9, 3.976698408423822*^9}, {3.976699509735929*^9, 
  3.976699652597845*^9}, {3.976773631155346*^9, 3.976773994478951*^9}, {
  3.9767740304393167`*^9, 3.97677437308587*^9}, {3.9767744103130198`*^9, 
  3.976774540438496*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"afcd9d2f-5d65-463d-a40b-\
6ea1964bb429"],

