Cell[TextData[{
 "Note that the rule now only 1. sets the convention of a single element, and \
2. implements the vanishing of elements not related to it by the assumed \
symmetry. These two statements are of course by far not themselves sufficient \
to determine all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["7", "7"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "13379a91-4510-4f13-8867-eb30b6d36860"],
 " = 823 543 elements. But if we impose complete antisymmetry, it is. \n\nFor \
more on exactly how this is done, see the Mathematica help pages. But \
importantly: as a user you don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t need to know exactly how it\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s implemented internally, you only need to understand how \
to specify the symmetries. \nFor higher dimension, the comparison goes like \
this:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.976697958963475*^9, 3.9766980210416403`*^9}, {
  3.9766990197238483`*^9, 3.976699113796857*^9}, {3.976699145236578*^9, 
  3.976699169176179*^9}, {3.976699370839941*^9, 3.9766994933397703`*^9}, {
  3.976774614643887*^9, 3.976774622237773*^9}, {3.9767746591596413`*^9, 
  3.9767746871757727`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ec4c0cbb-14d1-40c7-a262-\
9468b0bcc591"],

