Cell[TextData[{
 "So if we stopped at 3 dimensions, where the explicit array only uses 336 \
bytes and the symmetrized one uses 720 bytes, we might conclude that the \
explicit array is the most efficient! Instead, we see that the ",
 StyleBox["SymmetrizedArray", "Input"],
 " is ",
 StyleBox["by far",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " the most efficient for higher dimensions.\n\nYou might think, well 3 \
billion bytes for 9 dimensions is not so bad? But as you see, it is \
exponential: on my laptop when I ran this file the first time, I tried 10 \
dimensions, and it gave an \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]",
 StyleBox["out of memory error",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] for the explicit list storage. So if you ever \
needed to do anything that involved 10 variables (dimensions) or more, using \
an efficient form of storage can be the difference between being feasible and \
not being feasible. \n\nNote: physicists and engineers can get confused about \
the ",
 StyleBox["meaning of \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]dimension\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " here. If the real world has 3 or maybe 3+1 dimensions, who cares about 10 \
dimensions? Just think of \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]dimension\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] as the number of important variables in your \
practical problem. For example, for LLMs there can easily be millions of \
important parameters (or many more)! Not every important problem can be \
characterized by specifying 3 or even 4 variables."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.976692943142612*^9, 3.976692978857546*^9}, {
  3.976693023597909*^9, 3.976693033127314*^9}, {3.976693180019977*^9, 
  3.976693525811367*^9}, {3.97669803266352*^9, 3.976698117145009*^9}, {
  3.976774705294083*^9, 
  3.976774964889556*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c0c506e4-cc08-4554-919e-\
fbd39d7b61b3"]
},
